1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,200
Hi, I'm Oladapo Olaniyan. This video explores how my PepsiCo

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:11,400
valuation model works and how I checked it. The model

3
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:15,600
moves from filing inputs to assumptions, to forecast statements, free

4
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:19,800
cash flow, discounted cash flow value, and finally a value

5
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:24,000
per share.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:29,000
The valuation uses free cash flow to the firm, or

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,000
FCFF. Free cash flow to the firm focuses on cash

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,000
generated by operations before financing decisions. Starting with operating profit,

9
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:44,000
I apply taxes, non-cash items, capital spending and working capital

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,000
investment. I keep interest and other financing effects out of

11
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:54,000
the free cash flow to the firm so the cash

12
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,000
flow matches the enterprise value approach.

13
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:03,571
These are key assumptions. They include, but are not limited

14
00:01:03,571 --> 00:01:08,142
to, revenue growth, margins, capital spending, taxes, WACC, which stands

15
00:01:08,142 --> 00:01:12,714
for weighted average cost of capital, terminal growth, and terminal

16
00:01:12,714 --> 00:01:17,285
return on investment cost, ROIC. The model uses 7.66 percent

17
00:01:17,285 --> 00:01:21,857
WACC and 2.5 percent terminal growth in the base case.

18
00:01:21,857 --> 00:01:26,428
These are model inputs, not claims that the future is

19
00:01:26,428 --> 00:01:31,000
certain.

20
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,600
Here is the expected output. The base value is 117.29

21
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:44,200
dollars per diluted proxy share, compared with a recorded market

22
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:50,800
price of 125.65. The low, base and high outcomes are

23
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:57,400
76.12 dollars, 117.29, and 170.22 dollars, respectively. They are scenario

24
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:04,000
outcomes, not probability-weighted price targets.

25
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,500
The terminal value is 80.1 percent of enterprise value. That

26
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:15,000
means long-run assumptions have a large effect on the result.

27
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,500
This is why I avoid treating one discounted cash flow

28
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:26,000
number as enough evidence to buy the stock immediately.

29
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:32,571
These checks, as seen here, test whether the model works

30
00:02:32,571 --> 00:02:39,142
as documented. The run has 42 passes, zero failures, five

31
00:02:39,142 --> 00:02:45,714
open items, and two qualified checks. And, and I meant

32
00:02:45,714 --> 00:02:52,285
items. The checks cover statement connections, cash flow, the valuation

33
00:02:52,285 --> 00:02:58,857
bridge, sensitivity direction and known answer cases. Passing these checks

34
00:02:58,857 --> 00:03:05,428
shows the calculations run. It does not prove every forecast

35
00:03:05,428 --> 00:03:12,000
assumption is correct.

36
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,600
Finally, I ran the model in a clean environment. The

37
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:25,200
rerun reproduced the principal output files. That shows the project

38
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:31,800
can be rerun without relying on hidden local files. My

39
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:38,400
main limitation is still the economics behind the assumptions, especially

40
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:45,000
the current date bridge, peer comparability and long-run profitability.

41
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,822
All in all, this particular video shows us how my

42
00:03:52,822 --> 00:04:00,645
PepsiCo valuation model works and how I checked it. Thank

43
00:04:00,645 --> 00:04:08,468
you.
